    {
      title: "Munch Mate",
      description:
        "Multi-restaurant SaaS for table-QR, pickup and delivery orders, with a live view of the restaurant (isometric or 3D), inventory with recipes, plans and real-time rider tracking.",
      longDescription:
        "Munch Mate is a SaaS platform where each restaurant takes orders from the table QR code, for pickup and for delivery, all on a kitchen board that updates live. Customers follow their order in real time; for deliveries they watch the rider move on a map, with delivery zones drawn as polygons and validated by location. The dining room includes a table account with \"call the waiter\" and \"ask for the bill\", a voluntary tip, and split bills in equal parts, by item or by free amounts, each payment with its own method. Staff can edit an order before the kitchen starts it, while the server reprices it and notifies the customer.\n\nThe owner draws the restaurant's floor plan (tables, kitchen, pickup counter and delivery door) and gets a \"Live view\": the restaurant in isometric, generated as SVG from that plan, with each table colored by its account (ordering, eating, called the waiter, asked for the bill or paying in parts), kitchen tickets floating over the kitchen with a timer against the goal, pickups ready, scooters leaving on deliveries with a mini map, and sales and timing indicators updated in real time. Each area shows up only if the restaurant uses it. The plan supports several floors, terrain and decoration, and is shown in isometric (SVG) or, optionally, in 3D with Three.js: that engine is only downloaded when the user turns it on, after a notice that it uses more battery. People are animated from the real state: diners at occupied tables, the waiter walking to the table that calls and the cooks working while there are tickets.\n\nIt also covers scheduled product discounts, opening hours, roles (owner, cashier, kitchen, waiter and rider), daily sales and Excel export, PDF receipts, push notifications, WhatsApp/SMS notices and a Windows agent that prints kitchen tickets on ESC/POS thermal printers. An optional inventory deducts supplies from recipes per product and per option when an order is accepted, marks anything that runs out as \"Sold out\" automatically, sends push alerts and emails the shopping list every day. Plans (Mesa, Local and Completo), with a 30-day trial, are enforced on the server and managed from a platform admin panel. Staff can install the app on their phone and get push alerts for new orders and for tables calling the waiter. Self-hosted multi-tenant architecture: Next.js and NestJS, BullMQ queues on Valkey, Socket.IO, a MongoDB replica set, S3 storage with Garage and self-hosted OpenStreetMap maps (PMTiles), all in Docker Compose behind Caddy. Quality is backed by unit tests, Testcontainers integration tests, API end-to-end tests, Playwright browser flows and a smoke test against the production stack.\n\n⚡ AI-Assisted — Claude Code was part of the whole development: architecture and data model design, phased implementation with specialized backend and frontend subagents, security review (tenant isolation, CSRF, httpOnly cookie sessions, SSRF in notifications), tests and documentation. The screenshot walkthrough of this project is itself an automated Playwright script.",
